 \begin{abstract}
-Stochastic epidemic models often combine detailed contact networks within communities with aggregate information about contacts between them. We describe a two-scale simulator for this setting. Each community is simulated by a local Gillespie engine, while a macro layer generates cross-community infections from aggregate rates and places them uniformly among susceptible nodes. We specify the local event clocks, the import rule, and the grid-based scheduler. We distinguish the implemented scheduler from an ideal continuous-time process, which is exact for a surrogate network with uniform inter-community coupling. Comparisons with full-network Gillespie simulation examine how this approximation changes infection arrival times and their distributions. The aim is to identify conditions under which aggregate coupling reproduces the quantities of interest and to explain the sources of discrepancy.
